/-!
# The Ramanujam--Samuel theorem for a prime divisor

Source: EGA IV4, Proposition 21.14.3(i), second assertion; Guo, Divisors on coherent
schemes and homogeneous spaces, Proposition 3.3, proof of (iii) implies (i)
(blueprint: lem-sfb-ramanujam-samuel-prime-divisor).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u

open CategoryTheory

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData

/-- The reduced ideal of a codimension-one irreducible closed subset of a smooth scheme
is locally generated by a non-zero-divisor if it contains no component of any fibre.
Here `IsInvertible` is the effective Cartier ideal predicate, defined by precisely such
local generators, and `vanishingIdeal Z` defines the reduced induced closed subscheme.

Source: EGA IV4, Proposition 21.14.3(i), second assertion; Guo, Divisors on coherent
schemes and homogeneous spaces, Proposition 3.3, final paragraph of its proof
(blueprint: lem-sfb-ramanujam-samuel-prime-divisor).

Proof:
1. Smoothness and local finite type ascent make Y locally Noetherian
   (def-sbc-smooth-morphisms, pqf-def-locnoeth); lem-sfb-smooth-normal-ascent makes
   its local rings normal domains. The prime cycle [Z] has codimension one.
2. Let T consist of xi and the images of all generic points of fibre components.
   The latter avoid Z by the fibre hypothesis and closedness, so the cycle is zero
   there and has equation 1. At xi, lem-sc-normal-dim1-dvr makes the local ring a
   DVR; a uniformizer has multiplicity one and is an equation for the cycle.
3. A point of local dimension at least two whose closure misses T is not generic
   in its fibre. By lem-sfb-smooth-nongeneric-punctured-line-triviality every line
   bundle on its punctured local spectrum is trivial. Now
   lem-sfb-parafactorial-principal-cycle-extension extends the cycle equations
   from T to every point of Y. No global finiteness is needed for a single prime cycle.
4. At y in Z, the reduced ideal stalk is a height-one prime p in the normal local
   domain R, by lem-weil-pbc-reduced-induced-closed-subscheme. The cycle equation
   a has valuation one at p and zero at other height-one primes. The intersection
   formula of lem-per-divisorial-fractional-module-lattices implies a is in R and
   in p, and c/a is in R for every nonzero c in p. Thus p = (a), with a a
   non-zero-divisor. Outside Z the ideal stalk is the unit ideal.
5. On a Noetherian affine neighbourhood the reduced ideal I is finite. Represent
   a stalk generator by b in I after shrinking. The finite modules I/(b) and
   Ann(b) vanish at the point; kill their finitely many generators by inverting
   a product outside its prime. On this smaller neighbourhood I = (b) and
   Ann(b) = 0. This is exactly def-nagata-effective-cartier-ideal. -/
theorem isInvertible_vanishingIdeal_of_smooth_avoiding_fiber_components
    {S Y : Scheme.{u}} [IsLocallyNoetherian S]
    (hS : ∀ s : S, IsDomain (S.presheaf.stalk s) ∧
      IsIntegrallyClosed (S.presheaf.stalk s))
    (f : Y ⟶ S) [Smooth f]
    (Z : TopologicalSpace.Closeds Y) (hZ : IsIrreducible (Z : Set Y))
    (ξ : Y) (hξ : IsGenericPoint ξ (Z : Set Y))
    (hdim : ringKrullDim (Y.presheaf.stalk ξ) = 1)
    (hfib : ∀ (s : S) (C : Set (f.fiber s)), C ∈ irreducibleComponents (f.fiber s) →
      ¬ C ⊆ (f.fiberι s) ⁻¹' (Z : Set Y)) :
    (vanishingIdeal Z).IsInvertible := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData
